'\" t
.\" Manpage for bcx - terminal calculator for floating-point expressions.
.TH BCX 1 "2026-05-08" "bcx 1.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bcx \- terminal calculator for floating-point expressions with interactive REPL
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bcx
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.RB [ \-V | \-\-version ]
.RB [ \-s | \-\-scale " " N ]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.RI [ expression ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bcx
is a Bash wrapper around
.BR bc (1)
that fixes its poor terminal UX by providing readline command history,
clean Ctrl-C handling, an interactive REPL, and a quoteless-friendly
command-line syntax.
.PP
All non-option arguments are joined into a single expression and every
space character is stripped, so
.RI ` "bcx 23 x 42" ',
.RI ` "bcx 23x42" ',
and
.RI ` "bcx \(dq23 * 42\(dq" '
are equivalent in terminal mode.
.PP
In terminal mode, the literal letter
.B x
in command-line arguments is substituted for
.BR * ,
allowing expressions with multiplication to be entered without shell
quoting. The substitution does
.B not
apply inside the REPL, nor under command substitution
.RB ` $(...) '
or any pipe \(em those run in pipe mode, where
.B *
must be used directly (and shell-quoted to escape glob expansion).
.PP
Internally, bcx invokes
.B bc \-\-mathlib
so the math library functions
.RB ( s ", " c ", " a ", " l ", " e ", " sqrt )
are available, and the default scale is 20 fractional digits.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the help text and exit 0. The synopsis line reflects the actual
invocation name, so the help adapts when bcx is run via a symlink or
shell alias.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print
.RI ` "<name> <version>" '
and exit 0.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-scale " " \fIN\fR
Set bc's
.B scale
variable (fractional-digit precision) to
.IR N ,
a non-negative integer, for the duration of this invocation \(em both the
one-shot evaluation and any subsequent REPL session. Without this flag, bc's
mathlib default of 20 applies. An explicit
.RB ` "scale=M;" '
inside the expression still wins because it executes after the prepended
directive. Invalid or missing
.I N
exits with status 22
.RB ( ERR_INVAL ).
.TP
.B \-\-
End-of-options marker. Required before expressions that begin with
.B \-
(e.g.,
.RI ` "bcx \-\- \-3+5" ')
so a leading minus is not mistaken for an unknown option.
.SH MODES
.TS
tab(|);
l l l.
I/O|Args|Behaviour
_
Terminal|yes|Echo \fB> expr\fR to stderr, evaluate, then enter REPL
Terminal|no|Enter REPL immediately
Pipe/file|yes|Evaluate once, print result, exit (22 on bc error)
Pipe/file|no|Print help to stderr, exit 2
.TE
.PP
.B Terminal
mode is detected when
.B both
stdout and stderr are connected to a TTY
.RB ( "[[ \-t 1 && \-t 2 ]]" ).
If either is redirected, bcx runs in pipe mode.
.SH REPL CONTROLS
.TP
.B Ctrl-D
or empty line \(em exit (code 0).
.TP
.B Ctrl-C
\(em exit (code 130).
.TP
.B Up / Down arrows
\(em history navigation.
.TP
.B Ctrl-R
\(em reverse history search.
.PP
REPL evaluation errors are reported but do
.B not
affect the exit code \(em the exit status reflects
.I how
the session ended, not what happened during it.
.SH EXAMPLES
Quoteless terminal-mode usage (args joined, x \(-> *):
.PP
.nf
.RS
bcx 23x42                       # 966
bcx 1024 x 1024 x 1024          # 1073741824
bcx 22/7                        # 3.14285714285714285714
bcx 1.05^10                     # 1.62889462677744140625
bcx 100x1.05^10                 # 162.88946267774414062500
.RE
.fi
.PP
Quote when the expression contains parentheses, semicolons, or literal
whitespace inside a string:
.PP
.nf
.RS
bcx "sqrt(2)"                   # 1.41421356237309504880
bcx "scale=4; 22/7"             # 3.1428
bcx "a(1)*4"                    # \(*p via 4\(mu atan(1)
.RE
.fi
.PP
Override the default 20-digit scale with
.BR \-s / \-\-scale :
.PP
.nf
.RS
bcx \-s 4 22/7                    # 3.1428
bcx \-\-scale 0 7/2                # 3            (integer truncation)
bcx \-s 4 "scale=10; 22/7"        # 3.1428571428 (in-expr scale wins)
.RE
.fi
.PP
Capture output. Note: command substitution is pipe mode, so x \(-> *
is skipped \(em use literal
.B *
and quote it:
.PP
.nf
.RS
result=$(bcx "42*69*72/3.14")
.RE
.fi
.PP
Setup as a shell alias for quick access (bcx adapts its help/synopsis
to the invocation name):
.PP
.nf
.RS
alias ?='/usr/local/bin/bcx'

? 23x42                         # quick calculation
? "scale=4; 22/7"               # pi approximation
.RE
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Used to locate the persistent history file at
.IR $HOME/.bcx_history .
.TP
.B BCX_SCALE
Default value for
.BR \-\-scale .
Must be a non-negative integer. The command-line flag overrides; an unset or
negative value leaves bc's mathlib default of 20 in place. Useful for setting
a per-shell default, e.g.
.RB ` "export BCX_SCALE=4" '
in
.IR ~/.bashrc .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.bcx_history
Persistent REPL command history (HISTSIZE=1000, HISTFILESIZE=2000).
Written on exit when bcx is run in terminal mode.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal exit (REPL terminated via Ctrl-D / empty line, or one-shot
evaluation succeeded).
.TP
.B 2
ERR_USAGE \(em no expression supplied to a non-terminal invocation
(help printed to stderr).
.TP
.B 5
ERR_IO \(em could not create the temporary error-capture file.
.TP
.B 18
ERR_NODEP \(em the
.B bc
binary was not found on
.BR PATH .
.TP
.B 22
ERR_INVAL \(em invalid CLI option, or
.B bc
emitted a parse / runtime diagnostic in one-shot mode.
.TP
.B 130
REPL interrupted with Ctrl-C.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bc (1),
.BR readline (3)
.SH AUTHOR
Gary Dean \(em
.UR https://garydean.id
.UE
